A metric $d$ on a set $X$ is a function $d : X \times X \to \mathbb{R}$ such that for all $x, y, z \in X$:

1. $d(x, y) \geq 0$ and $d(x, y) = 0$ if and only if $x = y$;
2. $d(x, y) = d(y, x)$;
3. $d(x, y) \leq d(x, z) + d(z, y)$ (triangle inequality).

First we check that for all n, $n^{2} \mod 5$ equals 0, 1 or 4:

\begin{array}{cccccc}
n \mod 5 & 0 & 1 & 2 & 3 & 4 \\
n^{2} \mod 5 & 0 & 1 & 4 & 4 & 1 \\
\end{array}

Since $4 ≡ −1 \pmod{5}$ it follows that for every n, $n^{2}$ is congruent to $0, 1,$ or $−1 \pmod{5}$. Therefore $n^{2}$ is of the form $5k, 5k − 1,$ or $5k + 1$ for some integer k. In all cases $|n^{2} − 5k| ≤ 1$.

